window.ENT142_INDEX=[{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"How do we turn a social problem into a venture people use?","x":"Your team turns one real social problem, linked to an SDG, into a social venture with evidence. Topics: Overview of the Course · Course Material · Course Assessment · Revisiting the Design Thinking Process · Recap of the DTP · Step 4: Prototyping · Step 5: Testing. CLO 1 · CLO 3."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s1","n":"Course map","t":"Course material and assessment","x":"Half of your grade is Project 1: report and presentation on Tuesday 27 October, submitted on Moodle. You build it step by step in the Project 1 Lab."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s2","n":"Design thinking recap","t":"Recap of the design thinking process","x":"Design thinking has five steps used in loops: empathize, define, ideate, prototype, test. UAE example: InstaShop's founder found ordering groceries by phone slow, built a mobile-first app in Dubai (2015), and Delivery Hero acquired it in 2020, valued at $360 million."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s3","n":"Define your problem","t":"Activity · Define your problem","x":"Write your problem as [user] needs [need] because [insight], then turn it into a “How might we…?” question."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s4","n":"Step 4 · Prototyping","t":"Step 4 · Prototyping (preview)","x":"A prototype is the cheapest thing that lets a user experience your idea. Start low fidelity; Week 5 goes deeper."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s5","n":"Step 5 · Testing","t":"Step 5 · Testing","x":"Give one task, stay silent, watch, then ask open questions. Never defend the idea."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s6","n":"Test exchange","t":"Activity · Test exchange","x":"Next week: read Byers et al (2019). Ch. 1; each member brings 3 ideas linked to an SDG. Swap prototypes with the team on your left, test for 8 minutes each, and fill in the feedback grid."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Which idea deserves our next 12 weeks?","x":"Today each team chooses one idea and forms its final team. Topics: Idea Selection and Evaluation · How to Identify Effective Ideas · Discussion of Ideas · Group Formation. CLO 1 · CLO 3."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s1","n":"Effective ideas","t":"How to identify effective ideas","x":"An effective idea passes four lenses: desirability, feasibility, viability and impact, and it is an opportunity when the problem is real and the timing is right. UAE example: Kitopi (Dubai, 2018) saw delivery growing while restaurants were hard to scale, and started with a few small brands before bigger clients."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s2","n":"Pitch round","t":"Activity · Discussion of ideas: pitch round","x":"Pitch your idea in 60 seconds: problem → who → solution → why now. Listeners answer: I like · I wish · What if."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s3","n":"Score your ideas","t":"Activity · Score your top ideas","x":"Score your top ideas on the four lenses. The matrix supports the decision; if two are close, choose the one you can test fastest."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s4","n":"Form your team","t":"Activity · Group formation","x":"Next week: read Byers et al (2019). Ch. 4 and Blank (2013), HBR. Teams of 4–5, one lead role each: coordinator, data, customer, design, business. Register your team (CUD account) →"},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"How do we learn what customers want before time runs out?","x":"Plan the cheapest experiment that shows whether people want your idea. Topics: Lean Startups · Introduction to Lean Startups · Minimum Viable Product (MVP). CLO 1 · CLO 3."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s1","n":"Lean startup","t":"Introduction to lean startups","x":"A lean startup tests its guesses with real customers before building, in short Build → Measure → Learn loops (Ries, 2011; Blank, 2013)."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s2","n":"MVP","t":"Minimum Viable Product (MVP)","x":"An MVP gives the most validated learning with the least effort (Ries, 2011). UAE examples: The Entertainer sold printed two-for-one voucher books before launching its app in 2013; Careem launched in Dubai in 2012 as a corporate car-booking service before serving everyone. Click each MVP type for more cases."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s3","n":"Pivot or persevere","t":"Pivot, iterate or persevere?","x":"Set a target number before the test, then compare. Try different results."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s4","n":"Design your MVP","t":"Activity · Design your MVP experiment","x":"Next week: read the UN SDGs, Qian et al., (2024) and Bridge et al 2020 Ch1. Fill in your experiment card. The result becomes evidence in the Project 1 Lab."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Can a venture make money and make things better?","x":"Place your venture in the social economy, measure it on the triple bottom line and link it to an SDG target. Topics: Sustainable Innovation and Entrepreneurship · Introduction to the Third Sector and Social Economy · Recap of Triple Bottom-line Framework · Recap of SDGs and Linking the SDGs to the Product. CLO 1 · CLO 3."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s1","n":"Third sector","t":"Introduction to the third sector and social economy","x":"The third sector is mission-driven: charities, NGOs, cooperatives and social enterprises (Bridge et al., 2020). Examples: the UAE Food Bank (2017) and Grameen Bank, 2006 Nobel Peace Prize."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s2","n":"Triple bottom line","t":"Recap of the triple bottom line","x":"Elkington's triple bottom line (1994): people, planet, profit, each with a number you can track. Drag the sliders for your venture."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s3","n":"SDGs recap","t":"Recap of the SDGs","x":"17 goals, 169 targets, adopted in 2015. Link your product to a target (e.g. 12.3 on food waste), not just a goal."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s4","n":"Link to a target","t":"Activity · Link your product to an SDG target","x":"Next week: read Byers et al (2019). Ch. 8, Pradeep & Rajib (2022). Build the chain: product → output → outcome → measure → SDG target."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"How do we turn an idea into something people can experience?","x":"Make your idea visible, testable and easy to explain. Topics: Prototyping · Prototype Generation Techniques · Initial Prototype Development. CLO 1."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s1","n":"What is a prototype?","t":"A prototype makes an idea visible","x":"A prototype is a simple representation of your solution to see, explain and test it before full development. Jeff Hawkins tested the PalmPilot with a wooden block and a chopstick."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s2","n":"Six techniques","t":"Prototype generation techniques","x":"Six techniques: sketch, storyboard, paper, digital, physical, role-play. Click each for an example."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s3","n":"Prototype Helper","t":"Choose your prototype · Prototype Helper","x":"There is no single right prototype. Choose the fastest, cheapest, easiest one that tests your biggest assumption. Load an example idea, edit it, press Send."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s4","n":"Real prototypes","t":"How real teams prototype and show it","x":"Watch one video: notice how rough the first prototypes are. Startups demo first: prototype → problem → evidence → next test."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s5","n":"3D with Meshy AI","t":"A quick 3D prototype with Meshy AI","x":"For a physical product, Meshy AI turns a sketch or a sentence into a 3D model (free plan: 100 credits a month). Press ▶ Play. A model shows the look; only users show if it works."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s6","n":"Case: Embrace","t":"Case · Embrace infant warmer","x":"Stanford students built a low-cost infant warmer (2007). Observing rural families in Nepal changed the design: they added a home model."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s7","n":"Build v1","t":"Activity 2 · Build your initial prototype","x":"Answer the four questions, build the simplest version today, test it with another team. Keep photos."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s8","n":"Prototype → Project 1","t":"Project 1 starts here","x":"Next week: read Byers et al (2019). Ch. 4,7; bring your prototype photos. Your prototype is evidence in Step 8 of the Project 1 Lab. Report and presentation: Tuesday 27 October."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s0","n":"Overview","t":"Project 1 Lab · 11 steps to your report","x":"Task: Register your team, then work through the steps in order. Each step ends with a draft section and a check. Output: A complete, evidence-based report draft. Register your team (CUD account) →"},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s1","n":"1 · Venture card","t":"Step 1 · Venture card","x":"Task: Describe your venture in five fields. Output: Your one-line value proposition. Exploring first? Load the example: a fictional venture with real data."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s2","n":"2 · Verified data","t":"Step 2 · Collect verified data","x":"Task: find at least 5 numbers about your problem, customers and market on trusted sites. Add each as a data card. Where: UAE: fcsc.gov.ae · bayanat.ae · Dubai Data and Statistics Establishment · scad.gov.ae · ministries (*.gov.ae). World: data.worldbank.org · unstats.un.org/sdgs/dataportal · unep.org · who.int/data · ourworldindata.org. Research: Google Scholar, CUD library. Search: food waste site:gov.ae · \"statistical yearbook\" Dubai filetype:pdf. News and Wikipedia only to find the original source. Chatbots are not sources. Verify: who published it · year of the data · how it was measured ·"},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s3","n":"3 · Concept","t":"Step 3 · Concept and Vision","x":"Task: Answer the questions. Name two similar ventures and link one data card. Output: Concept and Vision draft + problem → solution → impact figure."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s4","n":"4 · Customers","t":"Step 4 · Customers and Customer Development","x":"Task: Describe your customer, link one card for how many people are affected and one for how serious the problem is, then show assumption → evidence → change. Output: Customer draft + customer profile figure."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s5","n":"5 · Market","t":"Step 5 · Market Analysis","x":"Task: Pick a verified total (TAM), set SAM % and SOM %, and the yearly spend per customer. Label assumptions. Output: Market draft + TAM/SAM/SOM figure with year-1 value."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s6","n":"6 · Competition","t":"Step 6 · Competition and Positioning","x":"Task: List 3 competitors (direct, indirect, substitute). Score everyone 1–5 on two things customers care about. Output: Competition draft + positioning map."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s7","n":"7 · Business model","t":"Step 7 · Sustainable Business Model","x":"Task: Enter who pays, prices, monthly volumes and costs, one people and one planet indicator, and your biggest risk. Output: Business model draft + revenue vs cost and triple-bottom-line figure."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s8","n":"8 · Prototype","t":"Step 8 · Prototype Development","x":"Task: Report your Week 5 prototype: assumption, test, result, version 2. Unsure of the technique? Use the Prototype Helper. Output: Prototype draft + v1 → test → v2 figure."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s9","n":"9 · Learning","t":"Step 9 · Learning and Adaptation","x":"Task: Each member completes their own three lines. Then answer as a team: is this a true opportunity? Output: One paragraph per member + team verdict."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s10","n":"10 · Intro + summary","t":"Step 10 · Introduction and Executive Summary","x":"Task: Describe your creative process and next step. The rest is built from Steps 1–9. Output: Introduction (max 250 words) + executive summary (max 1 page)."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s11","n":"11 · Assemble","t":"Step 11 · Assemble, check and submit","x":"Task: Copy the full draft into Word in the brief's order, expand it to about 4000 words, add cover page and contents, and tick every check. Output: Your submitted report."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s12","n":"Scorecard","t":"Validation scorecard","x":"Task: Read your score. Strengthen any red or amber line before you submit. Output: Evidence for “Is this a true opportunity?” (Step 9)."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s13","n":"Marking","t":"How it is marked","x":"Rubrics as written in the syllabus and the Week 5 briefing, plus the presentation brief."}];
